# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This reference is for Integration Hub team members configuring or investigating
actions requested after a file reaches the clean bucket. The file-action
adapter finds the most specific dispatch configuration for the destination
object key and publishes at most one `FileActionExecutionRequested.v1` event
for that configuration. Publication requests work; it does not confirm that a
downstream action or notification has completed.

## Dispatch configuration

Terraform creates dispatch secrets from `file_dispatch_prefixes` in
[`locals-dispatch.tf`](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/modernisation-platform-environments/blob/main/terraform/environments/integration-hub-file-transfer/locals-dispatch.tf).
The identity and configured prefix form an opaque full-object-key prefix. For
example, identity `example-transfer-identity` and prefix `/app-1/` match keys
beneath `example-transfer-identity/app-1/`.

Each secret contains an optional action and notification destinations:

```json
{
  "action": {
    "name": "place-on-sqs",
    "queueArn": "arn:aws:sqs:eu-west-2:123456789012:destination"
  },
  "notifications": {
    "email": "consumer@example.invalid",
    "slack": null,
    "teams": "https://example.invalid/teams-webhook"
  }
}
```

This illustrates the configuration shape, not a guarantee that an executor is
deployed. Action-specific configuration and routing rules are defined when
executors are introduced.

`action` is either `null` or an object with a non-empty `name`. Other action
properties are private configuration interpreted by the selected executor.
Each notification is either `null` or its private destination value. Null means
that action or notification is not configured.

The requested event contains only `action.name`, the names of configured
notification types and an immutable reference to the exact secret version.
Private action configuration and notification destinations stay in Secrets
Manager. A notification-only secret is valid. A secret with neither an action
nor notification destinations is a successful no-op.

## Prefix matching

The dispatcher tries the complete destination object key, then parent prefixes
at slash boundaries from longest to shortest. For
`identity/group/reports/test.csv`, it tries:

1. `identity/group/reports/test.csv`
2. `identity/group/reports/`
3. `identity/group/`
4. `identity/`

The first existing secret wins, including a no-op configuration. A missing
candidate is expected and lookup continues. If no candidate exists, processing
succeeds without publishing an event. An unreadable or malformed matched secret
fails the invocation, so Lambda retries and dead-letter handling apply; the
dispatcher does not fall back to a less-specific configuration.

Only clean `FileRouted.v1` events for the configured clean bucket reach the
dispatcher. Quarantine and investigation routes do not produce action requests.

## Delivery and idempotency

The adapter uses the source route event's `detail.metadata.idempotencyKey` for
Lambda-level deduplication. Replaying the same logical route under a different
EventBridge envelope ID does not republish a request while its idempotency
record exists.

`detail.data.actionExecutionId` is a deterministic UUID derived from the source
route idempotency key, matched secret ARN and exact secret version ID. The output
idempotency key is `action-request:{actionExecutionId}`. A different configuration
version produces a different execution identity when dispatch runs; rotating a
secret alone does not bypass the adapter's existing idempotency record.

The request retains the original `fileId` and `correlationId`, with the
`FileRouted.v1` EventBridge ID as `causationId`. EventBridge publication is at
least once: a retry can create a new envelope with the same logical request
identity.

Rules can route requests using `detail.data.action.name` or names in
`detail.data.notifications`. One request may fan out to multiple downstream
queues. Every consumer must independently deduplicate using `actionExecutionId`
or the equivalent `detail.metadata.idempotencyKey`.

## Security boundary

The dispatcher trusts the canonical `FileRouted.v1` input selected by its
EventBridge rule and validates the matched secret's JSON structure and action
name. Downstream executors interpret action-specific configuration by retrieving
the exact secret identified by `configurationReference.secretArn` and
`configurationReference.secretVersionId` in the event's `detail.data`.

Secret values must not be written to EventBridge, CloudWatch Logs or dead-letter
queues. Dispatcher logs contain only non-sensitive object identifiers,
secret-version identifiers, the action name and configured notification type
names.

